    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: Is This a Good Offer?\n\nThis is a **solid, standard baseline offer for an L6 PM** at Google, but it is **not compelling enough to leave a role where you are happy.**\n\n* **Leveling is a strict lateral:** Meta IC6 and Google L6 (Senior PM) are exact equivalents.\n* **Base salary is a step backward:** At \\$235,000, Google is offering you less guaranteed base cash than your current \\$240,000.\n* **Total Compensation (TC) increase is marginal:** At ~\\$526,000/yr at Meta vs. a steady-state average of ~\\$552,000/yr at Google (using equal-split vesting), you are looking at less than a 5% raise for taking on significant transition risk.\n* **No sign-on bonus:** Google typically uses sign-on bonuses to bridge unvested equity cliffs and incentivize passive talent.\n\n**Recommendation:** **Do not accept as-is. Negotiate aggressively.** You have significant leverage, and if Google will not meet your target, **walk away.**\n\n---\n\n### 2. Key Nuances to Watch Out For\n\n#### A. The Vesting Schedule Reality\nCheck whether your offer specifies **linear quarterly vesting (25%/yr)** or Google\u2019s **front-loaded schedule (33% / 33% / 22% / 12%)**.\n* If it is **front-loaded**, your equity payouts would be:\n  * **Year 1:** \\$356,400\n  * **Year 2:** \\$356,400\n  * **Year 3:** \\$237,600\n  * **Year 4:** \\$129,600\n* While Year 1 and Year 2 compensation would look high (~$638k/yr), Year 3 and Year 4 experience an **equity cliff**. \n\n#### B. The Recruiter\u2019s \"Refresher\" Pitch\n* **Never negotiate based on uncommitted refreshers.** Recruiters often present refresher stacking as guaranteed compensation, but refreshers fluctuate based on annual performance ratings (e.g., CME vs. EE), company-wide multipliers, and macro budgets.\n* Always anchor your decision on **guaranteed new-hire compensation**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Your Leverage\n\n1. **You are a passive candidate:** Google reached out to you; you are happy and performing at Meta.\n2. **High-performing Meta IC6:** You already possess strong equity stability and a known trajectory.\n3. **Room in the L6 Band:** The top of the L6 PM band at Google reaches **\\$620,000\u2013\\$680,000+** in Year 1/Year 2 total compensation.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What to Ask For (Your Counter-Offer)\n\nTo make a lateral move worth the risk, you should target a **15%\u201320% premium** in guaranteed year-over-year compensation plus a sign-on bonus to offset leaving Meta mid-cycle.\n\n| Component | Current Offer | What to Counter With | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | \\$235,000 | **\\$255,000 \u2013 \\$260,000** | Removes the base pay cut; posting range goes up to \\$271,000. |\n| **Equity (4-Yr GSU)** | \\$1,080,000 | **\\$1,250,000 \u2013 \\$1,300,000** | Increases annual average equity to ~$312k\u2013$325k/yr. |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | \\$0 | **\\$60,000 \u2013 \\$80,000** | Compensates for walking away from your unvested Meta RSUs/bonus. |\n| **Year 1 Target TC** | ~$552k \u2013 $638k | **~$680,000 \u2013 \\$720,000+** | Provides a clear financial premium over Meta. |\n\n---\n\n### 5. How to Frame the Negotiation\n\nReach out to the recruiter via phone (followed up by email) with a collaborative yet firm tone:\n\n> *\"I really enjoyed the process and was impressed by the team and scope. However, comparing this against my current compensation at Meta, the offer is essentially flat\u2014and actually represents a cut to my base salary.*\n> \n> *Because I wasn't actively looking and am in a great spot at Meta, leaving requires a package that makes clear financial sense to justify the transition risk.*\n> \n> *To move forward, I would need to see:*\n> 1. *A **base salary of \\$255,000**, which aligns with my current trajectory and the posted band.*\n> 2. *An **equity grant of \\$1.25M** over 4 years.*\n> 3. *A **sign-on bonus of \\$75,000** to offset the equity and bonus cycle I'd be leaving behind at Meta.*\n> \n> *If we can get the package to these numbers, I\u2019d be thrilled to sign.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 6. Walk-Away Threshold\n\n* **If they meet your asks (or come close):** Take the offer if you are genuinely excited about the Google team, scope, and product domain.\n* **If they refuse to move significantly** (e.g., offer remains under $1.15M equity with no sign-on and a lower base): **Politely decline and stay at Meta.** At IC6, you maintain strong standing and comp stability without taking on unnecessary lateral risk.",
